  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh-resolution models continue to indicate that shower and\nthunderstorm activity will increase late this evening just south\nof Lake Erie as an approaching trough and its associated cold\nfront move into the region. \n\nThe most likely window for storms to develop would be between\n7pm to 9pm near I-80. A few storms could be strong to severe,\nparticularly north of Pittsburgh, where a combination of modest\ninstability (900-1200J/kg of CAPE) and effective shear exceeding\n30kts will support organized convection. Damaging wind gust will\nlikely be the primary threat. However, a storm that accelerates\nsoutheastward could acquire some low-level rotation, potentially\nresulting in a funnel cloud or brief tornado. \n\nAs storms move toward the Pittsburgh area between 9pm and 11pm,\ninstability is expected to decrease, which could limit the\npotential for additional strong to severe storms. Lightning\nwould be the primary threat along with localized heavy rainfall. \n\nThe prolonged passage of the trough and lingering stationary\nboundary may support periods of moderate to heavy rainfall \ntotals on Thursday, potentially resulting in isolated flooding \nnear creeks and streams. \n\nDrier conditions are expected Friday under high pressure. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMachine-learning models suggest that after a brief period of \nridging on Friday, our large-scale weather pattern will \ntransition to a more zonal flow. As a result, this will allow \ndisturbances forming upstream (to our west) to track into our \narea. This pattern could create a heavy rain threat and/or a \nsevere weather threat early next week and will need to be \nmonitored.",
